Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
hi it's michelle vlad muffin beauty  science chemistry phd and cosmetic  chemist and today we're going to go  through my teenage skin care routine yes  a lot of the products were horrifying if  you like nerding out about the science  behind beauty products click like  subscribe and the notification bell i'm  turning 35 this year which means i grew  up in the 2000s the 2000s were a time of  britney flared jeans butterfly clips and  some really cringy skincare despite some  really cringe moments i think overall in  hindsight it was actually quite a nice  time  you had a bit more breathing room  between apocalyptic events  so here is what i look like in the 2000s  i went through a lot of regrettable  years when i didn't have a fringe i sort  of was channeling  the middle-aged accountant vibe  you can also tell it was like the height  of my trichotillomania i was doing a lot  of hair pulling and you can see the hair  regrowth is  a bit warped so what skin care was  teenage michelle using  firstly i was using the avon  invigorating scrub my dad bought this  when i started getting pimples and it's  kind of exactly what you'd expect a  clueless dad to buy this was bright blue  it had scrubbing particles in it which  were shaped like shards like they were  literally triangles  it was mentholated to death  and yeah it was fresh to death it felt  like you were sandpapering your face and  then dunking your face in a bucket of  ice water and this was kind of what we  assumed acne care should have been at  the time this was before the gospel of  paula of paula's choice our lord and  savior really took hold this was even  before google took hold we were  searching things on like yahoo search  alta vista and ask g's we weren't going  to find polar i'm pretty confident this  was way worse than sinai's apricot scrub  to no one's surprise my skin did not  like this i actually remember the skin  on my nose going hard and stiff and  cracking and i remember trying to peel  off the flakes with a pair of tweezers  and that was not good i ended up with  just a constantly scabby nose this was  dehydrating as hell and i have  dehydration prone skin  so yeah this was not a happy time you  cannot successfully treat acne by just  scrubbing it and drying it out harsh  treatments will usually just make acne  worse because one of the contributing  factors to acne is inflammation now the  next step in my teenage skin care  journey was clean and clear's three-step  oil control pack at the time clean and  clear had this massive campaign that was  targeting teenagers they would stick  these little sample bottle packs to the  front of teenage music magazines i got a  bunch of different sets from this  magazine i used to buy called tv hits  and i actually remember buying this  exact one it had five on the cover my  best friend at the time was obsessed  with this boy band 5 and she made me  give her the centerfold  we're not friends anymore for  unrelated reasons so looking this up  based on the ingredients it looks like  the exact same formulas as i was using  20 years ago are still available now the  packaging is exactly the same it doesn't  like they're sold in the us or the uk  anymore maybe they're just shunting the  leftovers to us australians because no  one cares about us okay the cleanser  this ingredient list looks nothing like  anything else i've seen in a really long  time i think the way it's working is  they've got free fatty acids and  triethynolamine which is a base  and by mixing that together they  basically make soap i remember this  being really foamy and for these soaps  of factors to foam well it'd have to be  somewhere near ph 8 or 9. it also has  methyl isothiazolinone as a preservative  this is mi and it's a pretty common  allergen it's only allowed in wash-off  products these days and you don't really  see it in face products that much  anymore it is reasonably common in  shampoos and conditioners my skin is  generally pretty robust but this  particular ingredient and its cousin mci  both make me really itchy  and i wonder if i was actually allergic  to this product at the time and i just  didn't know i do remember this being  pretty stripping and we all thought back  then that stripping is good type means  clean and a lot of people still think  this it's not true if you feel tight  after cleansing usually this means that  the proteins in your skin have changed  their shape they're denatured and since  these proteins are an essential part of  your skin barrier that means that your  skin barrier is probably leaky it's  losing water and it's letting irritants  in now the toner this was a really blue  alcoholic toner this is basically  paula's nightmare toner these days  toners are a lot better they're not just  dehydrating alcohol-laden products  anymore except apparently this one it's  a pretty simple formula it's got alcohol  and salicylic acid it's also got  glycerin 26 which is emollient it's sort  of like a less effective version of  glycerin it's also got alan tone and  panthenol which are soothing and  moisturizing ingredients which i don't  know if they're gonna help given  everything else in this formula this was  super watery so you had to apply it with  a cotton ball and i remember it stung so  much and the assumption was if it hurts  it works now the moisturizer this  ingredient list actually looks pretty  similar to a lot of moisturizers that we  have on the market now and it's actually  kind of nice seeing all these parabens  in there i remember this being fine i  remember i didn't really use it that  much because i had oily skin and acne  and the myth is if you have oily skin  and acne you don't need a moisturizer  i need a moisturizer i wonder if i would  have had an easier time getting into  skincare if this had glycerin in it it  does have propylene glycol as the second  ingredient this is a humectant but it's  nowhere near as hydrating as glycerin my  skin is really dehydration prone and  yeah this moisturizer did not convince  me that my skin needed moisturizer if i  was redesigning this moisturizer i'd  replace the propylene glycol with  glycerin and i would add sun protection  to this i think it doesn't have sun  protection because it has 0.5 salicylic  acid and in the us that would make it a  drug and i don't think you're allowed to  have sunscreen and salicylic acid in the  one product we already have salicylic  acid in the toner anyway so i think  getting rid of that and putting in sun  protection would be really good as an  introduction to teenage skin care i also  used clean and clear blotting papers and  these were also attached to the front of  a magazine these actually look pretty  much the same as the ones i used when i  was a teenager i don't think they've  really changed these and  yeah i think that's actually because  they were pretty good they're these  light blue sheets that you press down on  your face they soak up oil and you can  see the blue plastic go clear it's very  satisfying it's a nice way of getting  rid of excess oil without irritating  your skin  these are made of plastic though and  these days i use paper ones that i get  from an asian supermarket and they seem  to soak up more oil as well as being a  bit more environmentally friendly the  next step up was when i actually bought  my own products instead of getting them  off the front of a magazine  i was using cedarfield gentle skin  cleanser and nivea visage cleansing gel  i think these two products are where i  got my hate of cleansing over the sink  from the cedar fill was a gel product  that didn't foam so this one you could  either put it on your face and wash it  off or you could actually put it on your  face and then wipe it off with a damp  cloth since my skin was oily i kind of  felt like this just didn't do much and i  don't remember using it more than a  couple times i do remember the bottle  sitting in my bathroom for years so i  think really the problem here was that  this just didn't match my skin type  now the nivea cleansing gel some not  very well formulated cleansers do this  thing where they stick onto your hand  and they don't really foam up so you'll  put it in your hand you'll try to firm  it up you'll wash your face you'll rinse  off your face and then after all of that  you'll still have like  a dollop of gel stuck to the middle of  your hand even these days there are a  few cleansers i've used that still do  this one is the inky salicylic acid  cleanser i don't know why i kept on  buying this even though it was such a  pain to use i think it might have just  been the cheapest cleanser from the  supermarket but this was actually quite  hydrating it didn't strip my skin  it was just a little bit annoying to use  so onto the product that every teenager  ends up using benzoyl peroxide my first  benzoyl peroxide product was benzac 10  cream because i decided to be a cheap  boss and i saw this stuff online about  how you should be using 2.5 but i  figured 10 divided by 4 if i just dilute  that i will end up with 2.5  of course the first day i got lazy and  instead of diluting it i just sort of  went i'll just put less on my face and i  put it all over my face i went to uni  and then in the middle of the day  someone was like michelle  are you sunburned  so basically the benzoyl peroxide was  really irritating and i think it made my  skin all so sensitive to the sun and so  i just sort of had this beet red face  that just started in the middle of the  day to make things worse benzaak also  has micro sponge technology so it's got  like little white bits in it and i had  tiny little white flecks all over my  face as well it was a disaster so after  trying to make that work for a few days  i think i abandoned it and i just went  off and ordered 2.5 benzoyl peroxide  from acne.org acne.org is a website  started by a guy who had bad acne  and he made his own products because he  just found them so difficult to find and  2.5 benzoyl peroxide is like the  cornerstone product so his version was  really good it was pretty cheap it had a  decent texture and the only problem was  that it took a while to ship to me which  was why i started with the benzack this  worked quite well but it did sort of dry  the crap out of my face benzoyl peroxide  is really good at reacting with dyes and  breaking them up and that causes things  to bleach these days there are lots of  benzoyl peroxide washes available and i  would go with one of them instead  because you can wash it off and so less  of it ends up on your towels and on your  clothes and on your pillowcase so that  takes me to uni and the end of my  teenage years i still hadn't worked out  a skincare routine by that point  and honestly i didn't really get  anywhere with my skincare until i was  around 21 or 22 and even then it took me  a few years to get the hang of how to  give myself a skincare routine so it  took me till my mid to late 20s to  really understand my skin and be able to  predict which products would actually  work well for it my excuse is that we  didn't really have the resources that we  do now we didn't have skin care on  youtube the only place you could really  find good information was the pooler's  choice website also notice the distinct  lack of sunscreen in my routine i didn't  really start trying to wear it till my  mid-20s and even then it took me a few  years to get consistent with wearing it  every day so i hope you enjoyed this  throwback episode let me know if you  recognize any of these products or if  you use them and if not what were the  iconic products that you used in your  teenage years i'm going to post a  routine that i wish i had when i was a  teenager soon so subscribe and click the  notification bell for that you can also  follow me on instagram and tick tock and  check out my blog for more see you next  time for more science

